WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.095
<v Jamie>SpaceX just priced its IPO, and there's been hints at a Tesla merger before the stock even opens for trading.

00:00:07.095 --> 00:00:28.043
<v Alex>So if a Tesla merger is being floated, that's a pretty bold move. Gwynne Shotwell, the actual president of SpaceX, has been hinting at it—but the fact that she's even signaling it tells you everything you need to know about what happens when you hand someone a blank check and they immediately start shopping for a sports car they already own.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/12/spacex-president-gwynne-shotwell-just-gave-another-hint-at-a-tesla-merger/

00:00:28.043 --> 00:00:51.694
<v Alex>Markets were closed today, so we're looking at Friday's close: the S&P finished at seven-thousand four hundred thirty-one, up half a percent. The Nasdaq climbed point three-one percent. Small caps outpaced both—the Russell two-thousand jumped point seven-nine percent. Meanwhile, the V I X collapsed nine percent, which is the kind of fear gauge reset you see when traders wake up and realize they've been panicking about nothing in particular.

00:00:51.694 --> 00:01:05.884
<v Jamie>So risk is back on the menu, but there's some actual damage underneath. Adobe got hammered—down six point seven-six percent. Eli Lilly fell two point four-one percent. Tech and pharma getting their teeth kicked in while everything else parties. What happened?

00:01:05.884 --> 00:01:31.900
<v Maya>Adobe's earnings were fine, but the guidance was soft, and investors basically decided that if the software giant can't promise growth in an AI boom, nobody can. Lilly's a different story—some noise around G L P-one competition, but honestly the real move was A M D exploding four point seven-three percent because chip stocks decided Friday was their turn to be the cool kids, and when the cool kids show up, the pharma kids go home.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:47.442
<v Maya>Okay, so Shotwell's been signaling the merger, but here's what's wild: SpaceX's prospectus doesn't break down what percentage of revenue comes from government contracts versus commercial launches versus Starlink. Like, the company's about to trade and retail investors have no idea what they're actually buying.

00:01:47.442 --> 00:02:04.336
<v Alex>So the merger signal is either a negotiation play with the board or a very expensive way to change the subject. If SpaceX's margins are actually worse than the market assumes, a Tesla tie-up becomes the distraction that costs shareholders equity to solve a problem they didn't know existed.

00:02:04.336 --> 00:02:14.810
<v Jamie>But Shotwell herself is saying this. She's not some random board member—she's been running the company operationally for years. Why would she keep signaling something that Elon already walked back?

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:38.799
<v Maya>Because she might actually want it. Shotwell's been there for years, and a merger with Tesla means her operational expertise suddenly matters in a way it doesn't if SpaceX stays independent and Elon just keeps launching rockets and tweeting. She gets to be the adult in the room instead of the person who has to explain to investors why a launch provider's cash flow doesn't look like Amazon Web Services.

00:02:38.799 --> 00:02:44.205
<v Maya>SpaceX's prospectus still won't tell you if they're a government contractor or a space company.

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:49.611
<v Alex>Retail investors are about to own a mystery box wrapped in a Tesla press release.

00:02:49.611 --> 00:02:54.341
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
